{
  "id": "halden-offsite-power-restoration",
  "title": "Halden loss of offsite power and restoration",
  "description": "A summer night: Halden's four independently simulated PWR units export about 4.4 GW through their dedicated 420 kV switchyard, tied by three circuits each to Hasle and Tegneby (NO1). Two minutes in, a busbar fault at the switchyard trips the four turbines and opens all six circuits. The switchyard has no voltage-forming source of its own, so its ports de-energise and the Plants lose offsite power. Losing about 4.4 GW at once, three times the Nordic dimensioning incident of 1,450 MW, pulls the frequency below 49 Hz, and under-frequency load shedding disconnects part of Norway's demand. Restore offsite power by closing a circuit from the grid onto the dead switchyard, close the remaining circuits under synchro-check, then bring the grid back: raise hydro output and reconnect the shed load stage by stage. There are no weather-driven faults.",
  "objectives": [
    "Before the fault, note the export of the four unit bays, the switchyard voltage and the loading of the Hasle and Tegneby circuits and the NO1 corridors (connection-points.list, power-flow.snapshot).",
    "When the switchyard is isolated, confirm that its bays are de-energised (no voltage, no frequency) and that the Plants report loss of offsite power; follow the island frequency, the LFDD stages that operate and the reserves.",
    "Restore offsite power: close one Hasle circuit onto the dead switchyard (dead-bus closing), check that the bays are energised and the Plants draw station service from the grid, then close the remaining five circuits; synchro-check limits the voltage-angle difference across each loop you close.",
    "Restore the grid: raise hydro output until the frequency is back within the ±100 mHz standard range, then reconnect the operated LFDD stages one at a time (restore-lfdd-stage) without another under-frequency excursion, and re-arm them (rearm-lfdd-stage)."
  ],
